# ir_diff: programs where the two x86 paths disagree, held so the count cannot grow. Since the slice-9
# flip (#980) the sides are the DEFAULT, which selects from the shared IR, and `x86-legacy`, the legacy
# emitter alone (`ir_diff: sides default=ir other=x86-legacy`). MEASURED on native x86_64 (GitHub gate
# run 43310061101, part ir) at #1604 restacked onto main aa45ef19, Stage1 built by the seed: corpus 2623
# programs, with-ir=756, ir-fns=1354, agree=751, disagree=5 (build=4 exit=1 stdout=0), verify-failed=0,
# dump-failed=1 (test/issue926_const_qualified_same_name_ok.al, the front-half crash every path has,
# #1567, recorded in scripts/known-deviations.tsv).
#
# It was 4 and it is 5 because the flip's #1579 fixture is new: every row below is the LEGACY path
# being wrong, and the default answers what the fixture states and what aarch64, riscv64 and wasm
# answer. A row leaves when the legacy emitter goes (docs/ir.md §7.2), or by being fixed there.
#
#   legacy refuses, the default runs (the four #1022 stopgaps, `## expect: run N` in each file):
#     test/deep_field_agg_enum_reject.al                legacy build 1   default run 42
#     test/deep_field_agg_var_reject.al                 legacy build 1   default run 42
#     test/packed_in_plain_struct.al                    legacy build 1   default run 20
#     test/reject_local_name_two_aggregate_types.al     legacy build 1   default run 109  (#406)
#   legacy answers a value no arm computes, the default traps (`## expect(x86_64): trap`):
#     test/issue1579_match_no_arm_traps.al              legacy run 255   default run 132  (#1579)
#
# It was 5 and it is 6 because #1622's fixture is new (gate run 43468442478 on 51477418, part ir, the
# only new row): legacy reads a narrow-element `Slice(T)` over its word-spaced array at T's own stride
# (#1514, the silent wrong value this row records), while the default builds every function of the
# program from the shared IR, lays the array out at the element's stride (Types §6.4) and answers the
# fixture's `## expect: run 42`:
#     test/issue1514_word_array_range_slice.al          legacy run 1     default run 42   (#1514)
#
# It was 6 and it is 7 because #1626's fixture is new (gate run 43579579804 on 40850a18, part ir, the
# only new row): legacy copies an array parameter / array field wrongly (`ys = xs`, `ys = s.arr`), and
# the IR track takes no legacy-x86 fixes (#980); the default builds the program from the shared IR and
# answers the fixture's `## expect: run 42`, as aarch64, riscv64 and wasm do:
#     test/ir_array_param.al                            legacy run 1     default run 42   (#1626)
#
# It was 7 and it is 8 because #1632's fixture is new (gate run 43642090774 on 38751828, part ir, the
# only new row): legacy passes an `in` array argument as the caller's own array, with no copy (#1637,
# the legacy `in` aggregate copy), so `mix(a, a)` sees the write through its `in out` parameter in its
# `in` one; the default builds the program from the shared IR, copies (Functions §4.1) and answers the
# fixture's `## expect: run 42`, as aarch64, riscv64 and wasm do:
#     test/issue1632_in_array_copy.al                   legacy run 1     default run 42   (#1637)
#
# It was 8 and it is 9 because #1576's `str` fixture is new (gate run 43639251359 on d7ac6f88, part ir,
# the only new row): Stdlib appendix §3.6 (OP-9) makes `s[i]` on a `str` trap when byte `i` continues a
# code point, and legacy x86 emits no such check (#1550) and answers the continuation byte, while the
# default builds both functions from the shared IR and traps as the fixture's `## expect: trap` states,
# as aarch64, riscv64 and wasm do (the IR track takes no legacy-x86 fixes, #980):
#     test/ir_str_index_mid_codepoint.al                legacy run 169   default run 132  (#1550)
#
# It was 9 and it is 11 because #1723 builds `deref(p)` read whole on the shared IR (gate run
# 43982404790 on 089936fd, part ir, the only two new rows; the IR track takes no legacy-x86 fixes,
# #980). Legacy x86 passes the first word of a struct read through `deref(<call>)` where the struct's
# address is due (#1345) and dies with SIGSEGV. The default copies the pointee out (Memory §4.3) and
# answers the fixture's `## expect: run 42`, as wasm does:
#     test/ir_deref_agg_call.al                         legacy run 139   default run 42   (#1345)
# Legacy x86 cannot see the enum type of `match deref(gp(q))` and refuses the build (#716). The default
# reads it from `gp`'s declared `-> ptr(mut C)` and answers the fixture's `## expect: run 42`:
#     test/reject_enum_match_generic_deref.al           legacy build 1   default run 42   (#716)
#
# It was 11 and it is 12 because #1725's review asked for package fixtures of a callee reached through
# an import alias (gate run 44019297309 on 9b44c338, part ir, the only new row). In the control package
# whose aliased callees' pointee types are the same in both modules, legacy x86 cannot see the enum
# type of `match deref(gete(q))` and refuses the build, as for #716. The default reads it from the
# callee's declared `-> ptr(mut E)` and answers the package's `## expect: run 42`:
#     test/package/ir1723_alias_deref_same/package.al   legacy build 1   default run 42   (#716)
#
# It was 12 and it is 16 because #1705 builds fixed arrays of struct and enum elements and
# uninitialized aggregate locals from the shared IR (measured on omen, native x86_64,
# `scripts/ir_diff.sh` on the #1705 branch: disagree=13 over 9, the four rows below the only new ones;
# the merge with #1723's rows is re-measured on the gate of #1725's merge with main). Each fixture states
# `## expect: run 42`, the default answers it, and aarch64, riscv64 and wasm answer it too; legacy x86
# refuses the aggregate-element array (the IR track takes no legacy-x86 fixes, #980), or gives the
# uninitialized `[P; 2]` a one-word slot and writes its elements over the frame (#1708):
#     test/ir_agg_arr_local.al                          legacy build 1   default run 42   (#1705)
#     test/ir_agg_arr_enum.al                           legacy build 1   default run 42   (#1705)
#     test/issue805_exhaustive_index_copy.al            legacy build 1   default run 42   (#1705)
#     test/ir_uninit_agg.al                             legacy run 39    default run 42   (#1708)
#
# It was 16 and it is 17 because #1743 (landed on main, 013da247) resolves an aliased callee's
# signature types in the module that declares it, so the shared IR now builds the enum package of
# #1725's alias pair as it already built the control (08d1af63 pins `main::rd` Built; GitHub gate run
# 44400508517 on the 0.4.0 promotion, part ir, the only new row). Legacy x86 still cannot see the enum
# type of `match deref(get(q))` and refuses the build with #716's diagnostic (measured on omen with
# the promoted Stage2, `x86-legacy`); the default answers the package's `## expect: run 42`:
#     test/package/ir1723_alias_deref_enum/package.al   legacy build 1   default run 42   (#716)
#
# ir_diff prints every row but #1626's and #1632's as UNEXPLAINED: its `oracle`
# (scripts/fixture_oracle.sh) reads only the legacy header shapes (`## build_reject`, `## Expected exit:
# N`) in the first 30 lines, never the `## expect:` directive these files state. #1626's and #1632's
# files also say `Expected exit 42` in their headers, so ir_diff attributes those rows itself
# (LEGACY-WRONG; attributed=2 unexplained=15). Every row is explained above, by its file's own directive.
#
# Lowering the number means fixing a row, not editing this file.
